## PART B — RS (Residual Solvent)

### B1. Croscarmellose Ethanol calculated at 5000 ppm (USP/ICH guideline reference) instead of the material's own 100 ppm specification

**Confirmed from the real IFF source.** Section 4.36.2 states Ac-Di-Sol's ethanol is *"typically less than <100 ppm. The agreed specification is nmt 100ppm."* Section 4.42 (WADA statement, a different section) separately states ethanol is used *"at levels <100 ppm, which is significantly less than the USP/NF guideline of < 5000 ppm."* These are two different sentences in two different sections — one is the material's actual spec (100 ppm), the other is a guideline comparison (5000 ppm) used only to argue the material is well within a broader industry limit.

**Root cause.** `_extract_nmt_ppm_exact` (or the equivalent NMT-extraction logic in `residual_solvent.py`) takes the LARGEST NMT-style number found across the combined extracted text for a solvent, without distinguishing which sentence each number came from. Since both sentences may end up concatenated in the same solvent's `raw_text` (RS's own `_merge_rs_pages` concatenates page excerpts together), the extractor sees both "100" and "5000" and picks the larger as "conservative" — but conservatism must only apply among the material's OWN candidate values, never between the material's own spec and an unrelated guideline comparison.

**Fix.** In the NMT-number extractor, before taking the max, exclude any numeric match whose immediate surrounding text matches the existing `_RS_POLICY_CONTEXT_RE` / `_RS_REGULATORY_LABEL_RE` guideline-reference patterns (already present in the codebase — "USP/NF guideline of," "significantly less than," "ICH Q3C guideline") UNLESS that same number is also directly attached to the material's own NMT/specification wording (per the already-implemented citation-vs-reference distinction: "Methanol (as per USP <467>): NMT 3000 ppm" is a spec citing its standard and must be kept). Concretely: "the USP/NF guideline of < 5000 ppm" has no NMT/spec wording attached to the 5000 itself — it is a bare guideline mention — so it is excluded; "the agreed specification is nmt 100ppm" IS the material's own spec wording and is kept.

**Why this cannot break other products.** This uses the exact same citation-vs-reference distinction already implemented and verified correct for the "Methanol (as per USP <467>): NMT 3000 ppm" case earlier this session — it is being applied consistently to a second real example, not introducing a new rule. A product whose vendor spec genuinely has no separate guideline-comparison sentence is entirely unaffected.

**Verify:** Croscarmellose Ethanol spec becomes 100 ppm (not 5000); exposure recalculates to 8 mg × 100/1e6 = 0.0008 mg, not 0.04 mg. Re-run Trila and confirm its 9 solvents (all correctly at their own NMT specs, several of which legitimately equal ICH limits) are unaffected by this change, since none of Trila's solvent statements carry a separate bare-guideline-comparison sentence alongside their spec.

